    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Disaster_Response_Logistics\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)  # Set time limit to 300 seconds\n\n    # Data\n    depots = list(range(3))  # D0..D2\n    units = list(range(4))  # U0..U3\n    supplies = list(range(3))  # S0..S2\n    days = list(range(1))  # Day0..Day0\n\n    demands = [[[17.35], [15.48], [31.03]], [[28.18], [27.59], [15.17]], [[49.63], [10.81], [39.3]], [[11.24], [11.13], [19.0]]]\n    depot_supplies = [[[33.07], [30.78], [40.1]], [[42.94], [24.84], [33.92]], [[38.03], [21.62], [49.83]]]\n    transport_costs = [[3.57, 4.25, 2.76, 4.64], [2.9, 4.16, 4.99, 4.31], [1.69, 4.13, 1.71, 3.85]]\n    risk_costs = [[149.07, 116.58, 68.5, 88.1], [198.85, 124.66, 199.96, 145.63], [67.17, 155.05, 71.71, 152.11]]\n    supply_volumes = [1.5, 1.6, 1.93]\n    penalty_costs = [96.75, 55.08, 64.44]\n    vehicle_cost = 10.0\n    vehicle_capacity = 100.0\n    total_vehicles = 20\n\n    # Decision variables\n    # Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Amount of supply s from depot d to unit u on day t\n    flows = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                for s in supplies:\n                    flows[(d, u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Flow_D{d}_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}: Shortage of supply s at unit u on day t\n    slacks = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                slacks[(u, s, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Slack_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Number of vehicles on route d->u on day t\n    vehicles = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                vehicles[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.INTEGER, name=f\"Veh_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}: Route d->u is active on day t (binary)\n    routes = {}\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                routes[(d, u, t)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Route_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize total cost (transport + penalty + vehicle + risk)\n    obj = quicksum(\n        transport_costs[d][u] * flows[(d, u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        penalty_costs[s] * slacks[(u, s, t)]\n        for t in days for u in units for s in supplies\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        vehicle_cost * vehicles[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    obj += quicksum(\n        risk_costs[d][u] * routes[(d, u, t)]\n        for t in days for d in depots for u in units\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Depot capacity constraints: Total outflow <= supply\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for u in units) <= depot_supplies[d][s][t],\n                    name=f\"DepotCap_D{d}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Demand constraints: Inflow + slack >= demand\n    for t in days:\n        for u in units:\n            for s in supplies:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(flows[(d, u, s, t)] for d in depots) + slacks[(u, s, t)] >= demands[u][s][t],\n                    name=f\"Demand_U{u}_S{s}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Vehicle capacity constraints: Total volume <= vehicles * capacity\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    quicksum(supply_volumes[s] * flows[(d, u, s, t)] for s in supplies) <= vehicle_capacity * vehicles[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"VehCap_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Route activation constraints: vehicles <= total_vehicles * route\n    for t in days:\n        for d in depots:\n            for u in units:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    vehicles[(d, u, t)] <= total_vehicles * routes[(d, u, t)],\n                    name=f\"RouteAct_D{d}_U{u}_Day{t}\"\n                )\n\n    # Fleet size constraints: Total vehicles <= total_vehicles\n    for t in days:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(vehicles[(d, u, t)] for d in depots for u in units) <= total_vehicles,\n            name=f\"FleetSize_Day{t}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Situation:\nA severe storm has disrupted ground access across the region. Landslides, damaged bridges, and intermittent security incidents are constraining movement along the main corridors. Supply Depot Alpha (coastal port), Supply Depot Bravo (air logistics hub), and Supply Depot Charlie (inland warehouse) hold mixed stocks of medical commodities, potable water, and emergency shelter materials. Receiving units include Field Hospital Lima, Relief Battalion Sierra (forward distribution), Civil Support Company Echo, and Community Distribution Node Delta (displaced population camp). Weather windows and route security coordination are required for convoy movements.\n\nMission:\nDeliver life-saving supplies from the depots to the receiving units within the current operational period while minimizing total operational cost and risk. Prioritize commodities and destinations where unmet demand carries the highest operational impact.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Supply and demand control: Shipments from each depot cannot exceed available stocks by commodity and period. Each receiving unit\u2019s demand by commodity must be met; any controlled shortfall will be penalized to reflect humanitarian impact.\n- Commodities and load planning: Commodity volumetric factors govern how much can be loaded per vehicle. Heavier or bulkier items reduce per-vehicle throughput and will be consolidated accordingly.\n- Transportation and routes: Movements are direct from depots to receiving units along designated corridors. Each movement incurs a route-specific transport cost. Activating a route (sending any vehicles) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing escorts, route clearance, and overwatch, regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route. This fixed security overhead must be weighed against expected delivery gains.\n- Fleet employment: A standardized fleet with uniform capacity is available. Each vehicle dispatched adds a per-vehicle operating cost. Vehicles may only travel on routes that are activated, and total vehicles assigned across all routes cannot exceed the fleet ceiling.\n- Risk and access: Routes through the valley remain exposed to rockfall and residual flooding; alternate upland tracks require engineer assessment. Security escorts are limited and will be assigned to a small number of priority corridors to contain fixed risk overhead.\n- Cost-minimization approach: Consolidate flows on the safest and most economical corridors to reduce the number of activated routes, provided capacity and delivery timelines are satisfied. Activate additional routes only when the expected shortage penalty outweighs the fixed risk cost plus incremental transport and vehicle costs.\n- Priorities: Push medical items first to sustain clinical operations, followed by potable water and shelter materials as capacity allows. Adjust allocations dynamically to reflect evolving access conditions and penalty-weighted shortfalls.\n- Assumptions: Single operational period planning; no transshipment; route conditions and costs as provided in the data annex.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\nUnit Demands (Units x Supplies):\nUnit\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n-----------------------------\nU 0        |  17.35 |  15.48 |  31.03\nU 1        |  28.18 |  27.59 |  15.17\nU 2        |  49.63 |  10.81 |  39.30\nU 3        |  11.24 |  11.13 |  19.00\nDepot Supplies (Depots x Supplies):\nDepot\\Supply | S 0 | S 1 | S 2\n------------------------------\nD 0        |  33.07 |  30.78 |  40.10\nD 1        |  42.94 |  24.84 |  33.92\nD 2        |  38.03 |  21.62 |  49.83\nTransport Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        |   3.57 |   4.25 |   2.76 |   4.64\nD 1        |   2.90 |   4.16 |   4.99 |   4.31\nD 2        |   1.69 |   4.13 |   1.71 |   3.85\nRisk Costs (Depot x Unit):\nDepot\\Unit | U 0 | U 1 | U 2 | U 3\n----------------------------------\nD 0        | 149.07 | 116.58 |  68.50 |  88.10\nD 1        | 198.85 | 124.66 | 199.96 | 145.63\nD 2        |  67.17 | 155.05 |  71.71 | 152.11\nPenalty Costs per Unit Shortage: [Supply S0: 96.75, Supply S1: 55.08, Supply S2: 64.44]\nVolume per Supply Unit: [Supply S0: 1.5, Supply S1: 1.6, Supply S2: 1.93]\nFleet Parameters: Cost 10.0, Capacity 100.0, Total 20",
    "LLM_description_template": "Situation:\nA severe storm has disrupted ground access across the region. Landslides, damaged bridges, and intermittent security incidents are constraining movement along the main corridors. Supply Depot Alpha (coastal port), Supply Depot Bravo (air logistics hub), and Supply Depot Charlie (inland warehouse) hold mixed stocks of medical commodities, potable water, and emergency shelter materials. Receiving units include Field Hospital Lima, Relief Battalion Sierra (forward distribution), Civil Support Company Echo, and Community Distribution Node Delta (displaced population camp). Weather windows and route security coordination are required for convoy movements.\n\nMission:\nDeliver life-saving supplies from the depots to the receiving units within the current operational period while minimizing total operational cost and risk. Prioritize commodities and destinations where unmet demand carries the highest operational impact.\n\nExecution/Concept:\n- Supply and demand control: Shipments from each depot cannot exceed available stocks by commodity and period. Each receiving unit\u2019s demand by commodity must be met; any controlled shortfall will be penalized to reflect humanitarian impact.\n- Commodities and load planning: Commodity volumetric factors govern how much can be loaded per vehicle. Heavier or bulkier items reduce per-vehicle throughput and will be consolidated accordingly.\n- Transportation and routes: Movements are direct from depots to receiving units along designated corridors. Each movement incurs a route-specific transport cost. Activating a route (sending any vehicles) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing escorts, route clearance, and overwatch, regardless of the number of vehicles dispatched on that route. This fixed security overhead must be weighed against expected delivery gains.\n- Fleet employment: A standardized fleet with uniform capacity is available. Each vehicle dispatched adds a per-vehicle operating cost. Vehicles may only travel on routes that are activated, and total vehicles assigned across all routes cannot exceed the fleet ceiling.\n- Risk and access: Routes through the valley remain exposed to rockfall and residual flooding; alternate upland tracks require engineer assessment. Security escorts are limited and will be assigned to a small number of priority corridors to contain fixed risk overhead.\n- Cost-minimization approach: Consolidate flows on the safest and most economical corridors to reduce the number of activated routes, provided capacity and delivery timelines are satisfied. Activate additional routes only when the expected shortage penalty outweighs the fixed risk cost plus incremental transport and vehicle costs.\n- Priorities: Push medical items first to sustain clinical operations, followed by potable water and shelter materials as capacity allows. Adjust allocations dynamically to reflect evolving access conditions and penalty-weighted shortfalls.\n- Assumptions: Single operational period planning; no transshipment; route conditions and costs as provided in the data annex.\n\nAnnex A - Logistics Data:\n{DEMANDS}\n{DEPOT_SUPPLIES}\n{TRANSPORT_COSTS}\n{RISK_COSTS}\n{PENALTY_COSTS}\n{SUPPLY_VOLUMES}\nFleet Parameters: Cost {VEHICLE_COST}, Capacity {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}, Total {TOTAL_VEHICLES}"
